{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"constant-arthritic-mongoose","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# The comment dedup key is NOT normalized the way the description send value is\n\n## Summary\n\nThe description path and the comment path have different convergence protections, and the missing\npiece is exactly the normalization half. Today this is LATENT — it costs nothing, because rebar does\nnot currently model any content transformation on the comment send path. Any real Markdown/wiki\nconverter activates it, and the symptom is duplicate Jira comments re-posted on every pass, forever.\n\nFiled from a peer session's round-trip experiment over 5,568 real rebar bodies, verified here\nagainst the code rather than taken on report.\n\n## The asymmetry, in code\n\n**Descriptions are protected.** `adapters/jira_family/outbound_mapper.map_fields_to_remote` composes\n\n    normalize_outbound(fit_outbound(value))\n\nso the value sent is already its own fixed point under the round trip. The ordering is deliberate and\ndocumented in a long comment there, and it is PINNED by\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_rich_text_seam_heldout.py::test_cloud_send_path_composition_is_unchanged`,\nwhich includes an explicitly soft-wrapped case.\n\n**Comments are not.** `outbound_comments._diff_comments` builds its comparison key with\n\n    compare_body = sanitizer.fit_comment(_normalize_comment_body(raw))\n\nThere is no `normalize_outbound` equivalent. And `_normalize_comment_body` does NOT normalize both\nsides the same way — it calls `inbound_fields.normalize_rich_text`, which is:\n\n    if isinstance(body, dict):  return adf_to_text(body)     # the JIRA side: decoded\n    return str(body) if body is not None else \"\"             # the LOCAL side: pass-through\n\nSo the Jira side is decoded and the local side is handed back verbatim. The two sides are compared\nafter DIFFERENT transformations.\n\n## Why the truncation guard proves the shape of the bug\n\nThe comment path already carries one guard of exactly this kind, added after it bit:\n\n> the send path truncates an over-length body ... Apply the SAME shared truncation to the expected\n> local body before the membership test; otherwise the full local body never matches the truncated\n> Jira body and the diff re-emits forever.\n\nThat is the identical failure mode, patched for the ONE transformation the send path was known to\napply. Nothing generalizes it. A content transformation gets no such treatment, and the machinery\nthat would notice — a comparison against what Jira will actually store — does not exist.\n\nThe `_EXCLUDED_COMMENT_PREFIXES` canary exclusion is a second instance of the same class patched\nper-symptom: a volatile timestamp never matches a prior body, so the comment re-added every pass.\n\n## Why it is latent RIGHT NOW (do not \"fix\" a bug that is not firing)\n\nCloud comments go out through ACLI as `jira workitem comment create --body <text>`\n(`acli_cli_ops.add_comment`). rebar never computes the ADF that Jira will store, so it applies no\ncontent transformation of its own, and the dedup key happens to match. DC's `WikiTextCodec` is pure\nidentity in all four operations, so DC transforms nothing either.\n\nI could not establish whether ACLI's own text→ADF conversion alters bodies (e.g. rejoining soft\nwraps) — that is a live question about a third-party binary and only a live pass settles it. If it\nDOES, this is already firing on Cloud today. Empirically, rebar's own `text_to_adf` DOES rejoin soft\nwraps, so the transformation is not hypothetical for that encoder.\n\n## What I verified empirically (so the future work is not built on a wrong premise)\n\nProbing today's `adf.normalize_description` directly: `**/*.py`, `__pycache__`, `**a** **b**`, pipe\ntables, backticks, fenced blocks, `-`/`*` bullets and `__bold__` all round-trip BYTE-IDENTICALLY and\nare fixed points. Today's ADF path does no Markdown parsing at all — it only rejoins soft wraps and\nstrips trailing whitespace. So the peer session's 9-19% flap rate is a property of the PROPOSED\nconverter, not of anything shipping.\n\n## The good news: the seam is already in the right place\n\n`map_fields_to_remote` is SHARED and composes `normalize_outbound(fit_outbound(...))` for both\nbackends, so a pandoc-backed `WikiTextCodec` slots in by implementing the codec — no differ change.\nThe `RichTextCodec` four-operation split is what makes that possible. This ticket is about the\ncomment path catching up to the description path, not about redesigning the seam.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the comment dedup key is computed from what the SEND PATH will produce, the same way the\n      description value is — i.e. the local comparison key goes through the codec's outbound\n      normalization, not just `fit_comment`\n- [ ] a test asserts the property directly: given a codec whose outbound transform is NOT the\n      identity, a local comment already mirrored in Jira emits NO add. This is the cell that does\n      not exist today — every current dedup test uses transformations applied to both sides\n- [ ] a corpus/property test over real rebar comment bodies asserts encode-then-decode is a fixed\n      point for the comment path, not just unit examples\n- [ ] `RichTextCodec` implementations are pinned to `normalize_outbound(t) == decode_inbound(to_wire(t))`.\n      `WikiTextCodec`'s identity satisfies this trivially TODAY, which is why a pandoc implementation\n      that got it wrong would pass the existing suite\n- [ ] the dependency is DOCUMENTED: outbound has no origin-aware gate, and the reason it does not\n      need one is that degradation is symmetric. If encode ever stops being decode's inverse, a body\n      pulled from Jira starts being re-emitted and the human's Jira-native formatting is destroyed.\n      That invariant is currently load-bearing and unwritten","id":"a32a-4549-3eb9-4fb9","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Comment dedup key is not normalized the way the description send value is"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785779696498400001,"uuid":"2277b6dc-5e2e-4146-86f1-1df1083dc8d3"}